1. Rolling update
Suppose you have 3 Pods running version 1:
Deployment
   ↓
3 Pods → nginx:v1

You change the image to version 2:
nginx:v2

Deployment doesn't stop all 3 Pods at once.

It gradually replaces them:
Pod 1 → v1     Pod 2 → v1     Pod 3 → v1
                ↓
Pod 1 → v2     Pod 2 → v1     Pod 3 → v1
                ↓
Pod 1 → v2     Pod 2 → v2     Pod 3 → v1
                ↓
Pod 1 → v2     Pod 2 → v2     Pod 3 → v2

This is called a rolling update.

2. Rollback
Suppose version 2 has a problem.

You can tell Kubernetes:   “Go back to the previous version.”
Then Deployment can restore version 1.

v1 → v2
     ❌ Problem
     ↓
    Rollback
     ↓
v1

So simply:
Rolling update = safely move from old version to new version.
Rollback = go back from new version to old version if there is a problem.



If you mean the command to rollback a Deployment, use:      kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app

For example:   kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-deployment

Check rollout status    kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment
See rollout history     kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-deployment
Simple flow
Update image
    ↓
Deployment creates new ReplicaSet
    ↓
New Pods
    ↓
Problem?
    ↓
kubectl rollout undo
    ↓
Previous version



Example

First, Deployment is running:
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.26

This tells Kubernetes:  Change the nginx container image to nginx:1.26.

Then Kubernetes automatically does:
Old ReplicaSet
   ↓
Old Pods
   ↓
Gradually replace
   ↓
New ReplicaSet
   ↓
New Pods
Check the update    kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment

Another way

You can edit the YAML:
containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.26

Then:   kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Kubernetes detects the image change and performs the rolling update automatically.
Interview answer:
“When we change the Deployment's Pod template, Kubernetes automatically performs a rolling update. We can use kubectl set image to change 
the image and kubectl rollout status to monitor the rollout.”




















